{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"f3aea838f9bc6fd6","name":"card_unit_salamander","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Germany/WorldAtWar/units/card_unit_salamander","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"SALAMANDER","text":"Remove from the battlefield after it attacks until the start of your next turn.","flavorText":"The Heinkel He 162 Volksjäger, codename Salamander, was a jet-powered fighter developed at the end of WWII as Germany’s desperate answer to Allied air superiority. Built under the Emergency Fighter Program, it was constructed largely from wood in order to conserve metals. The compact aircraft had a top-mounted BMW 003 engine and a tricycle landing gear. Though designed to be simple enough for young pilots, its high speed and flight characteristics required experienced hands. Its development was rushed. The first prototype flew just 90 days after the design was selected in September 1944. Early flights exposed structural flaws due to poor adhesives used in wooden parts, leading to fatal accidents and design improvements. The He 162 entered limited service in 1945 with Jagdgeschwader 1. Operational use was brief and plagued by fuel shortages, structural failures and pilot inexperience. Of nearly 1,000 units put into production, only about 120 were delivered, and even fewer saw combat. Despite its shortcomings, test pilots, including some Allied ones, praised its flight handling. Production ended with Germany’s surrender in May 1945. Surviving aircraft were captured for testing, and several remain in museums today.","faction":"Germany","type":"fighter","rarity":"Unique","cost":4,"operationCost":1,"attack":2,"defense":3},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"damageToDeal":4,"Name":"card_unit_salamander","title":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_salamander_title","SourceString":"SALAMANDER","LocalizedString":"SALAMANDER"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::fighter","attack":2,"defense":3,"range":2,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Germany","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_salamander_text","SourceString":"Remove from the battlefield after it attacks until the start of your next turn.","LocalizedString":"Remove from the battlefield after it attacks until the start of your next turn."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_salamander_history","SourceString":"The Heinkel He 162 Volksjäger, codename Salamander, was a jet-powered fighter developed at the end of WWII as Germany’s desperate answer to Allied air superiority. Built under the Emergency Fighter Program, it was constructed largely from wood in order to conserve metals. The compact aircraft had a top-mounted BMW 003 engine and a tricycle landing gear. Though designed to be simple enough for young pilots, its high speed and flight characteristics required experienced hands. Its development was rushed. The first prototype flew just 90 days after the design was selected in September 1944. Early flights exposed structural flaws due to poor adhesives used in wooden parts, leading to fatal accidents and design improvements. The He 162 entered limited service in 1945 with Jagdgeschwader 1. Operational use was brief and plagued by fuel shortages, structural failures and pilot inexperience. Of nearly 1,000 units put into production, only about 120 were delivered, and even fewer saw combat. Despite its shortcomings, test pilots, including some Allied ones, praised its flight handling. Production ended with Germany’s surrender in May 1945. Surviving aircraft were captured for testing, and several remain in museums today.","LocalizedString":"The Heinkel He 162 Volksjäger, codename Salamander, was a jet-powered fighter developed at the end of WWII as Germany’s desperate answer to Allied air superiority. Built under the Emergency Fighter Program, it was constructed largely from wood in order to conserve metals. The compact aircraft had a top-mounted BMW 003 engine and a tricycle landing gear. Though designed to be simple enough for young pilots, its high speed and flight characteristics required experienced hands. Its development was rushed. The first prototype flew just 90 days after the design was selected in September 1944. Early flights exposed structural flaws due to poor adhesives used in wooden parts, leading to fatal accidents and design improvements. The He 162 entered limited service in 1945 with Jagdgeschwader 1. Operational use was brief and plagued by fuel shortages, structural failures and pilot inexperience. Of nearly 1,000 units put into production, only about 120 were delivered, and even fewer saw combat. Despite its shortcomings, test pilots, including some Allied ones, praised its flight handling. Production ended with Germany’s surrender in May 1945. Surviving aircraft were captured for testing, and several remain in museums today."},"operationCost":1,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Unique","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","EffectType":"ECardEffectTypes::Trigger","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartofTurn"],"hasBlitz":true,"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Germany/World_at_War/T_salamander.T_salamander","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Jaroslaw Wróbel","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/SALAMANDER.SALAMANDER","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":263.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_salamander_photo","SourceString":"An Heinkel He 162 Volksjäger during takeoff. Date unknown.","LocalizedString":"An Heinkel He 162 Volksjäger during takeoff. Date unknown."},"blowUpExplosion":{"ObjectName":"ParticleSystem'P_ExplosionAircraftL'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Effects/Explosions/Large/Aircraft/P_ExplosionAircraftL.201"},"blowUpCameraShake":{"ObjectName":"BlueprintGeneratedClass'CameraShakeHeavy_C'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/examples/GrenadePack/Blueprints/CameraShakeHeavy.0"},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackFight